  hetu: three verdict organs read the same claim and the Pāṇinian scheduler does not decide between them. This is not a tie to be broken: it is the fourth position, and the machine holds it open.
  nasta: the single verdict you asked for. It does not exist, and that is a fact about the three organs, not about this session.
  nasta: ORGAN A (1.1.1) NayaKosha/Obstruction reads: syad-avaktavyam
  nasta: bhanga : Position B4Avaktavya
  nasta: samata : satya (truth value only)
  nasta: NOT IDENTIFIABLE. They agree in TRUTH VALUE and differ in CONTENT.
  nasta: This is Durnaya_CollapseIffEveryNayaAgrees exactly: collapse is
  nasta: available iff every pair of fibres is equivalent, and `Mixed` with
  nasta: fibres Unit and Bool is the checked witness that both-inhabited is
  nasta: not enough. Any single verdict here discards the difference below.
  nasta: maximal collapsible sub-families (varga), by content: { #0 } { #1 }
  nasta: WHAT A SINGLE VERDICT WOULD DESTROY:
  nasta: from `episode-is-real (#1)`, 2:
  nasta: - the diagnosis at 39
  nasta: - the documented 2025 episode
  nasta: from `harm-is-real (#0)`, 2:
  nasta: - the Oct 2022 statements
  nasta: - the fear reported afterwards
  nasta: ORGAN B (1.1.2) Saptabhangi reads: syad-asti
  nasta: harm-is-real → syad-asti
  nasta: episode-is-real → syad-asti
  nasta: folded with sahaS → syad-asti
  nasta: NOTE what this organ holds that the others do not: `syad-asti` is a PROFILE. After jihvābheda the fourth position does not record which two seeds produced it, and that module states this is the doctrine's claim, not a modelling artefact — which is exactly why its saha does not associate.
  nasta: ORGAN C (1.1.3) SaptabhangiGarbha reads: syad-asti syad-asti
  nasta: sadhaka: harm-is-real <- the Oct 2022 statements [the statements themselves, dated]
  nasta: NOTE what this organ holds that the others do not: the fourth position here CARRIES both nayas and both witnesses (Sesa), and `caturthatTritiya` reads them back out. Where the profile organ says the seeds are consumed, this one says they are retained.
  nasta: DEFECT -- vipratiṣedha undecided at site 0.
  nasta: Two or more rules contend and no metarule decides. The scheduler does
  nasta: NOT choose: breaking this tie by list position would be a silent
  nasta: collapse (AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §६: transport or a written defect, no
  nasta: third path). A caller may fall back to pūrva -- the weakest of the
  nasta: five paribhāṣās -- to preserve an existing engine's behaviour, but only
  nasta: after this record exists, and it is then pūrva that is reported, not a decision. candidates:
  nasta: 1.1.1 NayaKosha/Obstruction — B1..B7 + ADharmin -- NayaKosha/Obstruction — B1..B7 + ADharmin
  nasta: 1.1.2 Saptabhangi — presence profiles + Apratipatti -- Saptabhangi — presence profiles + Apratipatti
  nasta: 1.1.3 SaptabhangiGarbha — proof-relevant, residue carries both nayas -- SaptabhangiGarbha — proof-relevant, residue carries both nayas
  nasta: metarules tried, in Paribhāṣenduśekhara 38 order:
  nasta: Apavada no candidate is an apavāda to all the others
  nasta: Antaranga the domain abstains: no nimitta depth is defined for these offers
  nasta: Nitya no candidate applies-after-the-other while the others do not (kṛtākṛtaprasaṅga is mutual or empty)
  nasta: Para the domain declares its rule order unauthored: a list index is not a position, so 1.4.2 has nothing to compare
  nasta: ŚEṢA RETAINED BY THE SCHEDULER — the object contended over: the claim
  nasta: offer 1.1.1 at site 0, width 1, result `syad-avaktavyam` — NayaKosha/Obstruction — B1..B7 + ADharmin
  nasta: offer 1.1.2 at site 0, width 1, result `syad-asti` — Saptabhangi — presence profiles + Apratipatti
  nasta: offer 1.1.3 at site 0, width 1, result `syad-asti` — SaptabhangiGarbha — proof-relevant, residue carries both nayas
  sesa: A ≃ B IS available and is exhibited by saptabhangi.samkramana, checked exhaustively over all sixteen round trips — those two may be identified and this machine identifies them
  sesa: C is NOT equivalent to either, in either direction, and saptabhangi.nasti computes the collision
  sesa: so the residue is exactly one distinction: whether the fourth position retains the two seeds that produced it. B says it does not, and calls that the doctrine. C says it does, and reads them back out with caturthatTritiya. That is the next question, and it is a question about Akalaṅka, not about Haskell.
  sesa: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §7: नयभेदे सङ्क्षेपो न विद्यते । न वर्जितः । न अनुचितः । न अशिष्टः । न विद्यते ।
  sesa: and a datum for that question, arriving from a third direction while this was being wired: the scheduler's own `Avaktavya` carried only a rendering (`Dosa`) when this machine first read it, and now carries a `Sesa` holding the contending offers ENTIRE — the scheduler lane reached `the fourth position must retain what produced it` independently, for its own reasons, and cites SaptabhangiGarbha's header in doing so. Two lanes arriving at C's position is not a proof of it; it is evidence about where to look, and it is recorded here rather than left in a diff.
  pramana: Pāṇini, Aṣṭādhyāyī 1.4.2 (vipratiṣedhe paraṁ kāryam), c. 500 BCE; Paribhāṣenduśekhara 38 for the order the metarules are tried in
  pramana: Akalaṅka, Laghīyastraya, c. 720–780 — sahārpaṇa, which is what B and C disagree about
  pramana: Siddhasena Divākara, Sanmatitarka 1.21 — a naya that asserts itself by denying another is a durnaya, which is what picking one of these three would be
